Ladybird checks the length that Function.prototype.apply, Reflect.apply,
and Reflect.construct read as soon as they read it, and throws before
reading any element if it’s longer than any call can take. The other
engines check the length at the same point: V8’s Object::
CreateListFromArrayLike() throws a RangeError for a length above
FixedArray::kMaxLength, WebKit’s sizeOfVarargs() throws a stack-overflow
error above maxArguments, and Gecko’s CreateListFromArrayLikeForArgs(),
in Reflect.js, throws a RangeError above MAX_ARGS_LENGTH. Ladybird
throws the InternalError it already threw for an argument list too long
for the interpreter stack, rather than a RangeError: A length below the
limit can still fail that way, and both mean the same thing.
